# CODEOWNERS: people or teams auto-requested when a pull request touches the
# matching paths. Review is still welcome from anyone; this file only decides
# who *must* approve, so keep it short and accurate.
#
# Every crate is maintained by the project author today. The per-crate entries
# below are the seams where a second maintainer is expected first, because the
# rendering, TLS and code-generation stacks each carry their own unsafe and
# security surface. When ownership is distributed, replace the catch-all with
# per-directory rules; a rule closer to the bottom wins.
#
# Syntax reference:
# https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners

# Everything not matched below.
*           @Tommicord

# Native code generation: per-architecture emitters are the riskiest merges in
# the tree, since a wrong encoding produces a bad instruction rather than a
# compile error.
/crates/ocl-native-codegen*/    @Tommicord
/crates/negex*/                 @Tommicord

# Cryptography and network protocol code. Changes here need a second pair of
# eyes on the side-channel and handshake-state rules in SECURITY.md.
/crates/tls/                    @Tommicord
/crates/wsocket/                @Tommicord

# `unsafe` lives mostly in the vectorized editors of the text engine.
/crates/wredit*/                @Tommicord
/crates/truetype-*/             @Tommicord

# CI, release and agent tooling: a broken workflow blocks every merge, so these
# files are owned explicitly rather than falling through to the catch-all.
/.github/                       @Tommicord
/.opencode/                     @Tommicord
